{"name":"North Island","hex":"#bcb6b4","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"North Island","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["An island, the northernmost of the two major islands making up New Zealand."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/North_Island"}],"entries":[{"title":"North Island","blocks":[{"text":"The North Island is one of the two main islands of New Zealand, separated from the larger but less populous South Island by Cook Strait. With an area of 113,729 km2 (43,911 sq mi), it is the world's 14th-largest island, constituting 43% of New Zealand's land area. It has a population of 4,067,400, which is 76% of New Zealand's residents, making it the most populous island in Polynesia and the 28th-most-populous island in the world."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/73/NewZealand.A2002296.2220.250m_North_Island_crop.jpg/330px-NewZealand.A2002296.2220.250m_North_Island_crop.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/North_Island","wikidata":"Q118863"},{"title":"North Island (Houtman Abrolhos)","blocks":[{"text":"North Island is the northernmost island in the Houtman Abrolhos, a coral reef archipelago in the Indian Ocean off the coast of Mid West Western Australia. Located about 14 km (9 mi) from the nearest island group, it is one of the largest islands in the Houtman Abrolhos, and one of the few to support dune systems. It has relatively diverse flora dominated by chenopod shrubs and fauna that includes the introduced tammar wallaby, around seven species of reptile, and about 15 resident bird species."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/Abrolhos_North-Island.jpg/330px-Abrolhos_North-Island.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/North_Island_(Houtman_Abrolhos)","wikidata":"Q1530387"},{"title":"North","blocks":[{"text":"North is one of the four compass points or cardinal directions. It is the opposite of south and is perpendicular to east and west. North is a noun, adjective, or adverb indicating direction or geography."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Compass_Rose_English_North.svg/330px-Compass_Rose_English_North.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/North","wikidata":"Q659"},{"title":"Island","blocks":[{"text":"An island or isle is a piece of land, distinct from a continent, completely surrounded by water. There are continental islands, which were formed by being split from a continent by plate tectonics, and oceanic islands, which have never been part of a continent. Oceanic islands can be formed from volcanic activity, grow into atolls from coral reefs, and form from sediment along shorelines, creating barrier islands. River islands can also form from sediment and debris in rivers. Artificial islands are those made by humans, including small rocky outcroppings built out of lagoons and large-scale land reclamation projects used for development."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Hawaje-NoRedLine.jpg/330px-Hawaje-NoRedLine.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Island","wikidata":"Q23442"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ce/Egmont_National_Park%2C_December_2015%2C_New_Zealand_%2842%29.JPG/500px-Egmont_National_Park%2C_December_2015%2C_New_Zealand_%2842%29.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ce/Egmont_National_Park%2C_December_2015%2C_New_Zealand_%2842%29.JPG/1280px-Egmont_National_Park%2C_December_2015%2C_New_Zealand_%2842%29.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wi